# {{.Name}}

Generated with the provisional Pluto CLI. Requires Go 1.25.5+. These app-local
primitives will be candidates for framework extraction; published Pluto Core, Auth and RBAC libraries
provide sessions and capability evaluation. Chi is the HTTP router, PostgreSQL/pgx + sqlc the database direction.

```sh
go mod download
go run ./cmd/api
# Another terminal: curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz
```

Authentication uses the pinned published pluto-auth module. `/healthz` is public.
Protected routes verify an opaque session, then resolve a principal through
`internal/access/memberships.go`. That authored resolver denies until implemented;
there are no default users or automatic role grants.{{if .Multi}} It must resolve
this exact subject and selected X-Tenant-ID; tenant headers never prove membership.{{else}}
Single tenancy does not use tenant headers.{{end}}

{{if .LocalCredentials}}
This project opted into local credentials (`capabilities.local_credentials=true`).
Startup requires DATABASE_URL and stops until you implement `localIntegrations`
in cmd/api/credentials.go. That authored function must supply a real Sender,
shared AttemptLimiter, trusted-client/concurrency Ingress and optional local
PasswordPolicy. There is no production no-op sender, demo password, admin
bootstrap, memory credential fallback or placeholder success.

Apply migrations 001 through 004 explicitly. `postgres.NewLocal` is the single
account/session backend and `transport.NewLocalAuthentication` builds the published
password.Service/passwordhttp adapter around it. Always use service.Sessions()
for local authentication, revocation and rotation; never issue local accounts
through session-only postgres.New or Manager.Start. Reset/disable fence stale
issuance and revoke existing sessions. authentication.Accounts exposes only trusted
Disable administration after application authorization; it is never a public route. Existing session-only providers can retain
the separate NewAuthentication path when intentionally configured.

POST /auth/enroll, /verification, /verify, /signin, /recovery, /reset and /logout
mount before tenant/membership and idempotency handling. Each requires an exact
trusted Origin, including unauthenticated flows and bearer logout. JSON is bounded
to 8 KiB and rejects duplicate/unknown fields and trailing JSON. Responses contain
only an opaque code; sign-in additionally sets a secure cookie. Enrollment,
verification resend and recovery return 202 without eligibility details; reset
requires reauthentication. Invalid credentials return 401, challenges 400, limits
429, dependencies 503. GET never consumes a challenge. Delivery failures accept
without an existence oracle; observe only the redacted DeliveryError callback and
ask for an explicit resend. Duplicate enrollment never resends or changes a hash.

The Sender must honor deadlines, build links only from fixed trusted HTTPS
confirmation destinations (prefer token fragments), and never log Notification or
persist plaintext challenge/outbox data. Provide real TLS/auth/config/timeouts at
the delivery boundary. Ingress must enforce authenticated proxy/client identity,
network limits, bounded concurrency/queueing and request deadlines before JSON/KDF;
never trust arbitrary X-Forwarded-For. Account limits must be bounded and shared
across replicas, with dependency failure denying requests. The library WindowLimiter
is process-local only. Active Argon2 work cannot be interrupted. Configure storage
abuse limits, abandoned-account retention, session/challenge pruning, DML-only DB
credentials and a maintained password blocklist if required. No production provider
or operational scheduler is bundled. Tests use bounded in-process delivery only;
the Pluto purchasing example demonstrates disposable loopback Mailpit delivery.

Enrollment owns only credentials; app membership resolution still denies until
you explicitly implement trusted provisioning. Never grant roles/tenant membership
from enrollment fields or email matching. There is no public admin bootstrap.
{{else}}
The composition root creates a shared session manager: bounded memory storage
for numeric-loopback development without DATABASE_URL, or the published pgx/sqlc
store with DATABASE_URL. Apply db/migrations explicitly; startup never creates
schema. Compose mounts migrations for a new development database; existing volumes
need new migrations separately. No credential verification/login route is mounted.
For external credential providers, verify credentials before Manager.Start, guard
browser login with HTTP.Guard, and use HTTP.WriteCookie. For local passwords use
`pluto init --local-credentials` and its fenced lifecycle instead.
{{end}}

GET /auth/session returns metadata and DELETE /auth/session revokes the server
record before clearing its cookie. Neither requires tenant membership. Cookies
remain Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax and host-only; browsers need HTTPS. Unsafe
cookie requests require an exact trusted Origin. Opaque bearer sessions are
explicitly enabled; mixed/duplicate credentials fail closed. Auth endpoints never
cache cookies/tokens through idempotency. Never log credentials or bodies.
AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS, AUTH_SESSION_TTL and AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT use the saved prefix;
defaults are the local development origin, 24h absolute and 30m idle. CORS, TLS,
trusted proxies and production integrations remain application responsibilities.
Incomplete business operations return 501. The root Auth module is stdlib-only;
password uses x/crypto. The current published PostgreSQL adapter contains the local
store in its package, so password/crypto are transitive dependencies even for a
session-only PG app. The CLI itself imports neither adapter nor password module.

```sh
pluto add endpoint widgets Create --method POST --path /api/widgets \
  --name 'Create widget' --description 'Create a widget after resource checks.' \
  --permission widgets.write --feature widgets-create --feature-client-visible
pluto add endpoint widgets Get --method GET --path '/api/widgets/{id}' \
  --name 'Read widget' --description 'Read a widget in the verified scope.'
pluto add endpoint reports List --method GET --path /api/reports \
  --name 'List reports' --description 'List authorized reports.'
pluto fmt
pluto vet
```

New apps include Docker, mise and fnox files by default. Init can omit each
independently with `--no-docker`, `--no-mise` or `--no-fnox`. Choices are saved;
endpoint additions do not enable omitted presets. Core lint/config/SQL files
are always included and Git hooks remain opt-in.

Commands discover pluto.toml in cwd/parents; saved tenancy/prefix are reused.
`pluto.toml` is project metadata, never runtime settings/secrets. Schema 6 includes typed job registrations and the shared Jobs worker foundation.
Unknown fields/settings fail; no automatic schema migration is implied.
For schema 2/3/4 apps, migrate authored app/config/main/transport wiring, module pins,
static paths, session storage and migration manually before updating the manifest.
The CLI refuses old schemas instead of overwriting authored authentication code.
For schema 4, review the new published pins, choose local_credentials explicitly,
compare the thin constructor/routes with a fresh scaffold, and apply 004 only if
local credentials are enabled before changing schema_version to 6.

Authorization imports `github.com/plutoframework/pluto-rbac` at the exact version
in go.mod/go.sum, with no sibling checkout or local replacement. Startup compiles
one immutable policy and injects it into the router and operations.
`internal/access/permissions_gen.go` declares capabilities and named endpoint
permission constants from the manifest. Authored `internal/access/roles.go`
starts empty: adding a permission does not automatically grant it to anyone.

After adding the sample widgets Create endpoint, edit `access.Roles()`:

```go
func Roles() []rbac.Role {
    return []rbac.Role{
        {
            ID:          "member",
            Name:        "Member",
            Description: "Create authorized widgets in the active scope.",
            Permissions: []rbac.PermissionID{WidgetsCreatePermission},
        },
    }
}
```

Role inheritance uses `Inherits: []rbac.RoleID{"member"}` on another named role.
Authored `access.Permissions()` can customize capability names/descriptions or
append capabilities outside HTTP. Unknown grants, parents or cycles fail startup.
The authenticator resolves `Principal.Roles` from verified server-side membership;
never trust an arbitrary role header/claim. A subject can have different roles
in different tenants. Route and direct operation checks use `principal.Can` with
the same injected policy. Unknown roles (even alongside known roles), absent
grants and a missing policy deny permission checks. Scope/ownership checks and
membership freshness remain application responsibilities. The library evaluates
trusted role IDs; this adapter uses the membership snapshot returned by auth.
`pluto fmt` and `pluto vet` validate the resulting app and exercise both boundaries.

Metadata in `[[endpoints]]` is the generation source for CLI-owned permissions, paths,
feature declarations and group/composition registries (`*_gen.go`). Do not edit
these files; vet checks their semantic content against the manifest. Route strings
appear only in paths at runtime. Models, per-endpoint handlers/operations/tests,
config and transport are authored files: the CLI creates them once and refuses
overwrite. Additions regenerate only managed files after checking prior ownership.
Manual catalog additions require taking over the generator contract deliberately.

`internal/handlers/<group>` only decodes input, reads verified context, calls a
consumer-owned operation interface and maps output. Business policies belong in
`internal/operations/<group>`, without HTTP or DB imports. Models are independent
wire types. Path parameters reach the operation via Request.Path, not JSON.
Inject real domain-specific repositories into operations when persistence is needed;
there is no universal database interface, ORM, resource authorization or durable
business audit implementation. Slog is diagnostic output, not durable audit.

Runtime defaults then explicit environment overrides, loaded/validated once and
injected. No implicit .env loading. `.env.example` documents {{.Prefix}} variables.
`FEATURE_<UPPERCASE_KEY>` normalizes dots/hyphens to underscores. Flags default
false; explicit false is preserved using pointers. Client-visible declarations
alone reach authenticated `/api/features`; route gates use the same evaluator.
These startup flags require restart and do not claim OpenFeature compatibility.
Feature visibility does not make an endpoint public or grant permissions.

Responses are `{data:...}` or `{error:{code,message,request_id}}`. JSON decoding
rejects unknown fields, invalid types, trailing JSON, and bodies over 16 KiB.
Known duplicate JSON keys use stdlib last-value semantics; bodies must be JSON
objects. GET/DELETE/HEAD/OPTIONS starters have no body contract. Add explicit
domain validation and resource policies; OpenAPI cannot infer them. The server
sets timeouts/security headers/no-store, negotiates gzip, and logs endpoint,
status, generated request ID and verified actor{{if .Multi}}/tenant{{end}}, never bodies or credentials.

Browser security defaults live in authored `internal/transport/security.go`,
registered globally before recovery/authentication. They apply to successful,
denied, missing, method-error and recovered-panic responses:

- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
  `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` and `Cache-Control: no-store`.
- CSP: `default-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none';
  frame-ancestors 'none'`, suitable for this JSON-only API.
- Permissions Policy disables camera, microphone and geolocation.
- COEP `require-corp`, COOP/CORP `same-origin`, and Origin-Agent-Cluster `?1`.
- DNS prefetch is off; legacy download/cross-domain policies are restricted;
  `X-XSS-Protection: 0` disables obsolete browser XSS filters.

These are inspired by [Fiber Helmet](https://docs.gofiber.io/next/middleware/helmet/),
implemented with net/http and no additional dependency. Adjust the authored
middleware deliberately if serving HTML, interactive documentation or embeddable
resources. CSP controls this API response document, not a separately hosted UI.
CORP limits cross-origin no-cors resource loading; it does not configure CORS.
Cross-origin frontend access still requires a reviewed CORS origin allowlist.
Set HSTS at your HTTPS terminator after choosing the domain policy. The local
HTTP scaffold neither sets HSTS nor trusts forwarded protocol headers; it never
opts a domain into includeSubDomains or preload. Headers do not replace
authentication, authorization, input validation or CSRF defenses for cookie auth.

```sh
go test ./...
go vet ./...
go run ./cmd/openapi -check
go test ./internal/transport -run '^$' -fuzz '^FuzzDecode$' -fuzztime=3s -parallel=2
pluto fmt                 # formatters + wsl fixes + formatters
pluto vet                 # nonmutating; appropriate for CI
pluto vet --staged        # conservative index/working-tree policy; see below
```

Install golangci-lint 2.15.0 from its official releases and put it on PATH, or
use `mise exec golangci-lint@2.15.0 -- pluto vet`. The CLI requires the exact tool
version and fails with guidance if absent. `.golangci.yml` is configurable; its
80-column golines target permits indivisible strings/comments. Vet also enforces
architecture imports (transitively), file sizes, manifest/generated contracts,
OpenAPI freshness, Go vet and tests. Do not compress code to evade size checks.
No formatter or import check can prove every business policy.

Git init runs only outside existing worktrees and never invokes user hooks.
Hook installation is opt-in (`pluto init --hook` or `pluto hook install`). It
refuses existing pre-commit hooks, honors core.hooksPath and never changes Git
config. Installing from a nested project refuses to alter ancestor hooks. An
installed hook invokes `pluto vet --staged` from the resolved project root.
Staged policy: refuse any unstaged, untracked or ignored project files, unresolved index
entries and staged symlinks; verify indexed regular files against their raw Git blob hashes, then vet the
identical working-tree contents. Ignored .env/bin files must be outside the
project for hook use; clean/smudge transformations that differ are refused.
No stash/index rewrite or claim to check a different staged snapshot. Stage all
project files before committing; reinstall the CLI on PATH for the hook. Hooks
are bypassable convenience; CI should run pluto vet.

Database foundation: optional {{.Prefix}}DATABASE_URL opens and pings pgxpool at
startup, then closes on shutdown. No operation uses it yet. db/migrations and
queries provide only a schema metadata probe, not domain persistence. Regenerate:

```sh
sqlc generate  # pin 1.31.1; sqlc.yaml uses pgx/v5, version 2
```

SQL integration requires a real dedicated database; default tests make no SQL
integration claim. No migration runner or production pool tuning is supplied.
{{if .Mise}}
`mise.toml` pins Go, golangci-lint and sqlc and supplies run/fmt/check/openapi
 tasks. Review before `mise trust; mise install`. Install pluto locally first.{{end}}
{{if .Fnox}}
`fnox.toml` has only a nonsecret local log-level value. Run
`fnox exec -- go run ./cmd/api` to scope environment injection to the child.
{{if .Mise}}`mise run run` supplies this command with pinned fnox 1.43.0.{{else}}Install
fnox 1.43.0 on PATH; this project omitted mise.{{end}}
For real secrets, install age, generate a key outside the repo with
`age-keygen -o ~/.config/fnox/age.txt` (do not overwrite an existing key), and add
`[providers.age]`, `type = "age"`, `recipients = ["<your public age recipient>"]`.
Set top-level `default_provider = "age"`, then `fnox set {{.Prefix}}DATABASE_URL --provider age` uses a hidden prompt and
writes ciphertext. Remove the plaintext local value/provider first; keep private keys outside
Git and arrange key access separately in CI. `fnox check --all` validates without
dumping secrets. Never commit .env/private keys or run env dumps for diagnosis.
See https://fnox.jdx.dev/guide/quick-start and provider docs.{{end}}
{{if .Docker}}
`docker compose up --build --wait` starts API + PostgreSQL using local-only
example credentials, loopback published ports 8080/55432, no privileged sockets
or host database mounts. API binds 0.0.0.0 inside its container; authentication
still fails closed. Compose healthchecks verify process liveness and pg_isready;
no domain SQL integration is implied. `docker compose down -v` discards local
state. Docker images use version tags, not immutable digests; review/pin digests
for deployment. Container checks require a running Docker daemon.{{end}}

Prototype boundaries: no external identity/delivery provider, RBAC management, durable audit,
domain SQL generator, distributed flags, production deployment, complete OpenAPI
schema inference (recursive types unsupported), or automatic migrations. No
network-free/offline promise: Go modules and optional tools may need downloads.
Generation preflights known conflicts/symlinks and rolls back its own file writes
on ordinary failures, but concurrent writers/process crashes are outside its
transaction guarantee. Keep a backup/version control for authored changes.

Per-endpoint `internal/app/<group>_<action>.go` bindings are authored constructor
choices. Change the operation constructor and this binding together when adding
repositories. Add typed fields to app.Dependencies and construct/inject them in
app.New. Generated group registries accept narrow Operations interfaces and never
choose concrete implementations; additions preserve authored constructor wiring.

## Pagination and idempotency defaults

GET List endpoints save collection intent and receive `pagination.Request` in
input.Page. Other GETs are not inferred as collections; use --collection or
--collection=false deliberately. Implement a tenant/ownership-filtered keyset
query with deterministic immutable sort and unique ID tie-breaker, SQL LIMIT
input.Page.FetchLimit(), then pagination.Finish. Return that shared page; the
handler encodes data/page metadata. Defaults are 25/max 100, no COUNT query.
Cursors are unsigned opaque/versioned boundaries bound to the verified actor,
{{if .Multi}}tenant,{{end}} operation and filters; they never authorize access.
If visibility depends on additional policy state, include it in the binding.

Authenticated business writes require Idempotency-Key (1–128 ASCII letters,
digits or -_.:) per intended action; reuse it for retries. Stable endpoint IDs
namespace verified subject{{if .Multi}}/tenant{{end}} keys after current auth,
RBAC and feature gates. Add --idempotency=exempt for sensitive/streaming responses.
Public writes and session lifecycle are exempt: public deduplication requires
an explicit authored safe application scope. Never wrap login/token responses.

DATABASE_URL wires durable pluto-core Postgres storage; explicitly apply
003_idempotency.sql. Without it protected writes return 503 until durable storage
is supplied. Tests inject bounded process-local memory; production never silently
falls back to it. Completed responses retain 24h; conflicts and pending claims
return 409. Uncertain requests remain blocked for operator reconciliation, even
past expiry; no automatic lease stealing. JSON responses are bounded to 256 KiB;
only Content-Type, bytes and status are replayed. Set-Cookie/auth/security headers
are not cached. Application 401/403/5xx remain uncached and leave pending claims.

The generic helper records completion after the operation and cannot guarantee
exactly-once external effects. To make database mutation/audit/completion atomic,
use core NewClaim/Store primitives and the Postgres adapter bound to the SAME pgx
transaction as typed application queries. See the published core README and
transaction example. Keep domain policy separate from these transport adapters.
For schema 3 migration review the CLI README; never overwrite authored source.

## Background jobs and transactional outbox

Pluto Jobs v0.1.1 / Postgres adapter v0.1.0 supply the runtime. Apply the explicit
`db/migrations/005_jobs.sql` migration before producers or workers run. Never run
DDL at startup. For session-only apps, migration 004 is intentionally absent.

```sh
pluto add job invoices Send --kind invoice.send
pluto fmt
pluto vet
# Export DATABASE_URL with the app prefix, then:
go run ./cmd/worker
```

Job payloads, handlers and unit/fuzz tests are authored files under
`internal/jobs/<group>`; TOML and `internal/background/handlers_gen.go` record
explicit registrations. Additional jobs preserve your existing handlers. Handler
stubs return an error until implemented; change the scaffold's test to assert
actual effects when implementing it. An empty worker refuses to claim any work.
Inject consumer-sized sender/repository interfaces in authored worker composition.

For transactional delivery, construct a typed request, then use the published
adapter inside your repository's existing business transaction:

```go
request, err := invoices.NewSend(
    invoices.SendPayload{ResourceID: invoice.ID},
    jobs.Options{TenantID: &verifiedTenant},
)
if err != nil { return err }
// tx is the SAME pgx transaction that writes the invoice and its audit event.
if err := outbox.EnqueueTx(ctx, tx, request); err != nil { return err }
return tx.Commit(ctx)
```

Construct outbox with `jobpostgres.New(pool)` at your composition boundary; import
`jobs` from github.com/plutoframework/pluto-jobs and `jobpostgres` from its
stores/postgres module. Application operations consume narrow repository
interfaces and never import pgx. Independent Enqueue cannot make a separate
business write atomic. Derive tenant metadata from verified scope; handlers must
load canonical records with tenant predicates. Keep raw passwords, auth tokens,
challenges and other secrets out of payloads. Credential delivery remains separate.

Worker defaults are explicit in nested `config.Config.Jobs`. The dedicated process
honors SIGTERM/interrupt and cooperative handler cancellation. Tune timeouts and
lease bounds together. Delivery is at least once, with finite attempts and no
heartbeat; make effects idempotent with job.ID or a stable domain event key.
Inspect fixed database diagnostics; do not log payloads or raw provider errors.
Applications own external senders, retention, monitoring and reconciliation.
{{if .Docker}}
The image includes api and worker. After implementing handlers, run
`docker compose --profile worker up --build`. Worker is an explicit profile so a
fresh app with no registrations does not start consuming/failing queued work.
{{end}}{{if .Mise}}
`mise run worker` runs the same entry point with your exported app environment.
{{end}}
Schema 5 migration is manual: compare a fresh schema-6 scaffold, add Jobs module
pins/sums, migration 005, worker/config/background files and an empty managed
registry before updating the manifest version. Preserve authored business code.

The mise sqlc pin uses the explicit Go backend instead of an unavailable registry
alias. sqlc 1.31.1 needs Go 1.26+ for generation; Go can fetch that toolchain without
changing the application's Go 1.25.5+ target or runtime dependency graph.
